MASTER PROMPT: EARNERS — PREMIUM SKILL LEARNING APP

Act as a senior product designer, mobile UX specialist, frontend engineer, backend engineer, accessibility expert, and QA engineer. Build a complete, production-minded skill-learning application named EARNERS.

The goal is to create a refined, realistic, highly functional learning platform inspired by the usability, navigation quality, and smooth learning experience of established education apps. Use original branding, original layouts, and original implementation. Do not copy another company's proprietary interface pixel-for-pixel.

The most important requirement: The finished product must NOT look AI-generated, vibe-coded, template-generated, or like a generic startup dashboard. It must look like a carefully designed, professionally hand-coded product that a real engineering team would confidently release.

Do not stop at a mockup. Build the actual application, implement working interactions, connect the backend where possible, test the important user flows, and fix errors before declaring the work complete.

1. Brand and Product Identity

- Application name: EARNERS
- Tagline: Learn Skills. Build Your Future.
- Category: Online skill learning and digital education.
- Audience: Beginners and learners interested in AI tools, video editing, graphic design, content creation, freelancing, technology, and other digital skills.
- Design direction: Premium Indian learning app, mobile-first, clear typography, practical course discovery, smooth navigation, and a trustworthy educational experience.
- Create an original logo and coherent design system for EARNERS.
- Never change the application name.
- Never use another company's logo, copied screenshots, proprietary assets, or misleading brand affiliation.

2. Anti-AI Design and Human-Crafted Product Quality

Treat this as a strict design requirement.

The application must feel intentionally designed and professionally implemented, not automatically assembled from a generic UI kit.

Avoid:

- Generic AI SaaS dashboard layouts.
- Excessive gradients, glowing borders, glassmorphism, and neon accents.
- Random floating blobs, unnecessary decorative illustrations, and excessive shadows.
- Repeating identical rounded cards for every piece of information.
- Oversized headings and excessive empty space.
- Emoji used as a replacement for professional icons.
- Fake statistics, invented testimonials, fake ratings, and fabricated user activity.
- Placeholder content, meaningless buttons, and unfinished screens.
- Excessive animations, distracting motion, and unnecessary effects.
- Unnaturally perfect but repetitive layouts that make every screen look identical.
- Random font combinations, inconsistent spacing, and inconsistent icon sizes.
- Default template styling left unchanged.
- Artificially generated course thumbnails containing distorted text.
- Fake loading delays added simply to make the interface look sophisticated.

Instead:

- Design every screen around its actual purpose.
- Use deliberate visual hierarchy, consistent spacing, and restrained colors.
- Use realistic course titles, lesson descriptions, and appropriate media assets.
- Use authentic-looking editorial course thumbnails with consistent art direction.
- Use a small, consistent icon family such as Lucide.
- Use clear labels and predictable navigation.
- Make touch interactions feel immediate and natural.
- Keep components reusable without making every page visually identical.
- Write clean, readable, maintainable TypeScript and CSS.
- Use semantic HTML, sensible component boundaries, and proper error handling.
- Add loading, empty, success, and failure states for real user operations.
- Ensure every visible control has a meaningful action.
- Make the interface look equally considered on small phones, large phones, tablets, and desktop screens.

Do not claim that code is hand-written if it was generated. Instead, achieve the visual and engineering quality associated with a professionally designed and maintained application.

3. Typography and Design System

Create a mobile-first design system with a professional, readable font.

Typography:

- Use Inter or Manrope as the primary UI font.
- Use a suitable system-font fallback.
- Keep headings, body text, labels, metadata, and buttons on a consistent type scale.
- Use appropriate font weights instead of making every heading bold.
- Ensure excellent readability at normal mobile viewing distances.
- Avoid decorative or artificial-looking fonts.
- Avoid overly tight line-height and inconsistent letter spacing.

Visual system:

- Use a restrained, premium color palette.
- Use a light neutral background with dark, readable text and a carefully selected brand accent.
- A warm yellow or golden accent may be used for important highlights, active states, or primary actions.
- Use neutral borders and subtle shadows only where they improve separation.
- Use consistent spacing tokens, border radii, elevations, and icon sizes.
- Ensure text and controls meet WCAG AA contrast requirements wherever applicable.
- Support accessible focus indicators and keyboard navigation.
- Support reduced-motion preferences.

Create reusable design tokens and components for buttons, inputs, course cards, navigation, dialogs, badges, progress indicators, tabs, skeletons, and alerts.

